  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Cuphophyllus borealis is a small, delicate waxcap mushroom characterized by its pale coloring and waxy gill texture. This northern species is part of the diverse Hygrocybaceae family, known for their distinctive waxy or gelatinous appearance.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Waxy Gills",
      "description": "Gills are thick, waxy, and widely spaced, ranging from white to pale yellow",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pale Cap",
      "description": "Small cap (1-3 cm) that is whitish to pale gray or cream-colored",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Slender Stalk",
      "description": "Thin, fragile stem that is typically white and matches the cap color",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Cuphophyllus borealis grows in moist grasslands, meadows, and moss-covered areas in boreal and northern temperate regions of New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Waxcaps like C. borealis have a unique waxy cuticle on their gills that gives them a distinctive slippery feel and helps them thrive in wet grassland environments.",
